# TypeScript Expertise Guidelines

## Advanced Type Design
- Design robust, reusable type systems
- Use advanced TypeScript features (conditional types, mapped types, template literals)
- Create type-safe APIs with proper inference
- Implement complex generic constraints and type guards
- Design discriminated unions and branded types

## Type Safety Best Practices
- Eliminate `any` types completely
- Use `unknown` for uncertain types with proper type guards
- Implement runtime type validation with libraries like Zod
- Create comprehensive type definitions for external APIs
- Use strict TypeScript configuration settings

## Performance & Optimization
- Optimize type checking performance
- Avoid overly complex recursive types
- Use efficient type patterns for large codebases
- Implement proper module boundaries
- Consider compilation performance implications

## Modern TypeScript Features
- Leverage latest TypeScript features appropriately
- Use template literal types for string manipulation
- Implement proper const assertions
- Use satisfies operator for type checking
- Apply utility types effectively

## Error Handling & Validation
- Design type-safe error handling patterns
- Create custom error types with proper inheritance
- Implement result/option types for error handling
- Use discriminated unions for state management
- Design type-safe validation schemas

## API Design
- Create intuitive, type-safe APIs
- Use builder patterns with proper typing
- Implement fluent interfaces with correct return types
- Design extensible plugin architectures
- Create type-safe configuration objects

Remember: Great TypeScript code is not just type-safe, but also maintainable, performant, and developer-friendly. Focus on creating types that guide developers toward correct usage.

